00:00:15.759 --> 00:01:11.109 align:start position:0%
 
you  hi  hello  this is working  hello  next speaker is uh sangha mitradatta  hi thank you everyone for having me here  today  so today i'm going to talk about some  strategies for fair and reliable machine  learning  that are deep rooted in causality and  information theory in particular an  emerging body of work and information  theory  that is called partial information  decomposition so machine learning is  entering into every aspect of our lives  these days  such as hiring education lending etc  so while these models are really great  at learning all kinds of patterns  present in the data  but  blindly learning all these patterns can  sometimes lead to unintended  consequences  like propagating biases and disparities  with respect to gender race age  nationality etc  that can have adverse effects on  people's lives  and in many cases is also disallowed by  law  so when it comes to identifying  addressing legal disputes or even  informing policies and interventions  only identifying the final bias in a  model is not always sufficient  we really need to dig deeper  and understand  how the disparity arose and answer  another question that bridges the fields  of fairness  explainability and law  that is how can we identify and explain  the sources of disparity in machine  learning models  so as an example  let us consider the hiring of a software  engineer for a safety critical  application  we have some inputs that go into a model  and the model decides whether to hire or  not  now it has been shown that even if these  protected attributes like gender race  age etc are not  explicitly used as input to the model  the outputs can still uh be correlated  with these protected attributes because  the other features might be strongly  correlated with these protected  attributes  so and this also closely connects with  the idea of a disparate impact  but then sometimes certain features are  very important for a particular  application  like coding test score might be really  critical in the hiring of software  engineers  and there are also similar discussions  in law which says the disparate impact  may be exempt  if it can be justified by some  occupational necessity  like coding test score may be critical  for the hiring of software engineers  our weight lifting ability might be  critical in the hiring of firefighters  but then aptitude test may not be so  critical as in this landmark court case  of griggs versus duke power  so the question that i am interested in  is  given a choice of critical features  how do we quantify if the disparities  exempt or non-exempt  and towards answering this question  our main contribution is a systematic  measure of non-exam disparity  which is the bias that is not justified  by the critical features  so this is my problem statement or  notations that i'm going to talk about  right now  z is the protected attribute which  denotes  gender rays etc  then we will have some features that go  into a model and produce the model  output y hat  now the critical features will be  denoted by xc  and all the remaining features which are  non-critical or general are denoted by  xg  and we assume that all of these features  are generated from an underlying  structural causal model or data  generation process which involves all of  these latent random variables  now the question is given a choice of  critical features xc what is a good  measure of non-exam disparity  once we have such a measure it can be  used either for auditing that is to  compute and see whether a model is  showing non-exam disparity or not  or it could also be used during training  for instance as a regularizer with the  loss function  but before we get there the first  question is how do we quantify this  quantity  so one candidate measure is conditional  mutual information or conditional  independence  that is the model will be fair if the  output y hat is entirely independent of  the protected attribute z  conditioned on the critical feature  xc but in this work we also make a key  of observation  that conditional dependence can  sometimes falsely detect bias or  misleading dependencies  even when a model is causally  fair so to understand what i mean by  this  i'll show a counter example  so let's consider a causally fair model  here we have  the critical feature coding test score  which depends on  gender or race and the true inner  ability  then we have the aptitude test score  which happens to only depend on the true  inner ability  and that the model makes its decisions  only based on this true inner ability  you which is independent of gender or  race  so such a model is causally fair from  the definitions of causal fairness that  is the output y hat should not depend or  vary with z  when you fix all the inner abilities u  but if we naively condition on a  critical feature here we will falsely  get a dependency because the conditional  dependence is greater than zero here  so one desirable property for such a  measure of non-exam disparity is that  the measure should be zero when the  model is causally fair  so in summary conditional mutual  information or conditional dependence  does not satisfy the causal fairness  property  and this limitation actually leads us to  look into this emerging body of work in  information theory  which is called partial information  decomposition  which further decomposes conditional  mutual information into two quantities  unique information and synergistic  information  and this unique information happens to  satisfy our causal fairness property and  some others  so what is this unique information  to understand this we'll look at an  example  so we consider two very similar looking  scenarios  in one case  on the left the critical feature  xc depends on z but the output doesn't  depend on z  which is the gender or race  whereas on the example on the right  the output has some information about  gender z that is not in the critical  feature xc  which is not fair  so  conditional mutual information happens  to be same for both of these examples  but a desirable property is to be able  to distinguish between these two cases  and say that in the first case there is  no disparity whereas in the second case  there is  so unique information is defined as  follows  it is the minimum conditional mutual  information  when the marginals between  z y hat and z xc are preserved  but the joint distribution of the three  variables are allowed to  vary so if you're familiar with  simpson's paradox or even interested in  mutual information unique information is  essentially the  intersectional dependence between mutual  information which is just dependence  and conditional mutual information  and this unique information will go to  zero whenever either of them goes to  zero  and this quantity satisfies our property  one as well as some other desirable  properties  but then we look into some more nuanced  examples which are again non-faithful  causal models  where even unique information alone  might not be sufficient  for instance these kinds of scenarios of  mass discrimination where the output  might be like an xor of  gender and inner ability that is  your the candidates with men with high  inner ability and women with low inner  ability are being shown an ad whereas  the other two groups of people are not  being shown an ad  so this remaining part i might go a  little bit quickly but because of this  limitation of  unique information  we finally arrive at a causal measure  that  synthesizes both partial information  decomposition and causality  and this measure satisfies some of the  properties that i already discussed  as well as some natural monotonicity  properties as well  but this measure depends on the true  structural causal model or knowledge of  the true structural causal model  but the idea is that i believe that  even if this measure depends on the  causal model we first need there is  value in first finding a measure that  satisfies all the axioms however  difficult it might be to compute  because then it can serve as a benchmark  for other observational relaxations and  help us understand their pros and cons  and also this observational unique  information is actually good enough for  a lot of situations and only for the  case of the mass discrimination is where  we need to move on from the  observational unique information to this  measure that leverages both unique  information and causality  then we also have some impossibility  results that no purely observational  measure can satisfy all the desirable  properties  but nonetheless the partial knowledge or  assumptions about the causal  relationships they can correctly  quantify non-exam disparity  and then we perform some case studies  using these observational measures on  artificial and real data as well as some  analysis on  the cmu graduate admissions data sets  now i'll very briefly touch upon two  other problems that i am also interested  in  so one of them is this question of  understanding social media and filter  bubbles like  let's  say we a person gets to see ads that  they really like  if you like to read new york times you  only get recommendations from new york  times if you like fox news you only get  recommendations from fox news  so how do we diversify recommendations  in a manner that  a person's interests are also retained  and here these kinds of  exempt and non-exempt topic bias can  play a role in understanding  how should you diversify recommendations  another question that i am also very  interested in is the quantification of  fairness and accuracy tradeoffs  so in this work i use an existing  measure of fairness that is equalized  odds and i quantify the trade-off  between accuracy and fairness using  these tools called churn of information  so in summary  this work  my work  bridges between  several fields like causality and  information theory  to arrive at measures of fairness  and in future work i'm also interested  in uh understanding  how these techniques can be  applied uh how can we arrive at better  estimators for these quantities  as well as have broader conversations  like what is the right interpretation of  laws for algorithmic hiring  for which i also believe it is essential  to collaborate with lawyers and social  scientists  so thank you so much for  having me here  [Music]

